```java
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/orders")
class OrderController {
    private final OrderService orders;

    OrderController(OrderService orders) {
        this.orders = orders;
    }

    @PostMapping
    ResponseEntity<OrderResponse> create(
            @Valid @RequestBody CreateOrderRequest request) {
        OrderResponse created = orders.create(request);
        URI location = URI.create("/orders/" + created.id());
        return ResponseEntity.created(location).body(created);
    }

    @GetMapping("/{id}")
    OrderResponse find(@PathVariable("id") long id) {
        return orders.find(id);
    }
}
```

`OrderService`, `CreateOrderRequest`, and `OrderResponse` are application types. They are assumed here so the example can stay focused on the controller's HTTP mapping.

```java
@GetMapping("/orders/{id}")
OrderResponse find(@PathVariable("id") long id) {
    return orders.find(id);
}

@PostMapping("/orders/{id}/cancellation")
ResponseEntity<Void> cancel(
        @PathVariable("id") long id,
        @Valid @RequestBody CancelOrderRequest request) {
    orders.cancel(id, request.reason());
    return ResponseEntity.noContent().build();
}
```

`CancelOrderRequest` is the validated request DTO that carries the cancellation reason. The GET handler only retrieves the order, while the separate POST makes the requested state change visible in the HTTP contract. The exact cancellation design can also use a documented state update; the important rule is that cancellation must not pretend to be a safe retrieval.
